      <section class="notes-section wrap" id="project-notes" aria-label="Project notes">
        <div class="notes-intro">
          <h2>Inside the system:<br />How we solved technical manual retrieval</h2>
          <p class="notes-lede">Standard vector databases assume everything is a paragraph of clean text. Car manuals are the exact opposite.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="notes-list">
          <details open>
            <summary><span class="mono">A</span><span>The problem: Vector search is blind to diagrams and fuse tables</span><span class="summary-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
            <p>Automotive service manuals are filled with pinout schematics, torque specifications, and fuse tables. Naive text extraction completely destroyed table column alignments and ignored label text in diagrams. In early testing, standard dense embeddings only retrieved the correct diagnostic section 76% of the time, often missing critical warning indicator procedures.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><span class="mono">B</span><span>The architecture: Hybrid retrieval with an OCR fallback pipeline</span><span class="summary-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
            <p>We split ingestion into two tracks: standard paragraphs went to BM25 and ChromaDB embeddings, while page sections containing schematics or tables were routed through RapidOCR to reconstruct spatial key-value pairs before indexing. At query time, Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) combines keyword matches for exact part codes with dense embeddings for natural driver phrasing, followed by Cross-Encoder reranking.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><span class="mono">C</span><span>Trade-offs: Staying within the cockpit latency budget</span><span class="summary-icon" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
            <p>Running a cross-encoder model on embedded hardware adds 25–40ms of computational latency. To prevent lag in voice feedback, we decoupled intent classification: an ultra-lightweight asynchronous FastAPI router (&lt;5ms) determines immediately if the driver needs a vehicle manual lookup, climate control, or media, while prefetching candidate chunks in parallel.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

